{0 Analytics Engine}

{!Workero.Analytics_engine} writes data points to a
{{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/analytics/analytics-engine/}Workers Analytics Engine}
dataset. You read them back with the SQL API.

{1 Configure}

{@toml[
[[analytics_engine_datasets]]
binding = "EVENTS"
dataset = "events"
]}

{1 Use}

{@ocaml[
let handler req env _ctx =
  let open Workero in
  let events = Analytics_engine.of_env env "EVENTS" in
  let path = URL.path (Request.url req) in
  Analytics_engine.write ~index:path ~doubles:[ 1. ]
    ~blobs:[ Text (Method.to_string (Request.method_ req)) ]
    events;
  Response.text "ok\n"

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

- {!Workero.Analytics_engine.of_env} opens the dataset. It raises
  [Invalid_argument] when there is no such binding, or when the binding has no
  [writeDataPoint] method.
- {!Workero.Analytics_engine.write} records one data point. [index] is the
  sampling key, [doubles] are the numbers and [blobs] the strings or raw bytes
  that you query by.

[write] returns at once and has no result: the runtime writes the point after
the handler goes on. It raises {!Workero.Js_error} only when the runtime throws
while it takes the data point. See the
{{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/analytics/analytics-engine/limits/}limits}
on the number and size of fields.

{1 Test}

The binding is not available in a plain [workerd] configuration. The tests of
workero use a stub that records the points. See
[test/worker/services/analytics-binding.mjs].
